            <fr:title text="AllGather">AllGather</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter><html:p>An <html:em>AllGather</html:em> gets resources from all devices onto one device. In <fr:link href="/NZZ7/" title="TPU Architecture" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/NZZ7/" display-uri="NZZ7" type="local">TPU's</fr:link> <fr:link href="/UEA0/" title="Ring topology" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/UEA0/" display-uri="UEA0" type="local">Ring topology</fr:link>, this is done in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\frac {N}{2}]]></fr:tex> hops via each TPU sending its shard to the neighboring chip.</html:p><html:p>
Assuming we have <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> shards and the total bytes is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N]]></fr:tex>, then each chip must send <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\frac {N}{X}]]></fr:tex> bytes. If we have an ICI bandwidth of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W_{\text {ICI}}]]></fr:tex>, then for each chip to send to its neighbor will be <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[T_{1\text { hop}} = \frac {N}{X\cdot  W_{\text {ICI}}}]]></fr:tex>
Now each chip must send across <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> hops, which gives <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T = X\cdot  T_{1\text { hop}} = \frac {N}{W_{\text {ICI}}}]]></fr:tex>.<html:label class="sidenote-ref"><html:input type="checkbox" /></html:label><html:span class="sidenote">This does not change if we use the bi-directional bandwidth because then we have <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2N]]></fr:tex> bytes but only need to send at most <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\frac {X}{2}]]></fr:tex> hops!</html:span></html:p>
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(219, 216, 215);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(109, 103, 101);">Remark.</html:span> 
A super fun trick from <html:span class="link-reference" tid="Ex 3.2" refid="scaling-book"><fr:link href="/scaling-book/" title="How to scale your model" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/scaling-book/" display-uri="scaling-book" type="local">[scaling-book, Ex 3.2]</fr:link></html:span>: If you have an unused replicated dimension, then you can shard the replicated axis and perform an AllGather over both dimensions to double your bandwidth!
</html:div>
<fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>12</fr:day></fr:date><fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/RBEJ/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>RBEJ</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/RBEJ/</fr:route><fr:title text="AllGather costs the same at any device count"><fr:link href="/VSDZ/" title="AllGather" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/VSDZ/" display-uri="VSDZ" type="local">AllGather</fr:link> costs the same at any device count</fr:title><fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="anki">1786546736461</fr:meta><fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p><fr:link href="/VSDZ/" title="AllGather" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/VSDZ/" display-uri="VSDZ" type="local">AllGather</fr:link>: signature, mechanism, and cost?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
<html:p><html:em>Signature</html:em> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[A_X, B] \to  [A, B]]]></fr:tex> — it removes a subscript, reassembling shards so every device on that axis holds a full copy. It can drop just one of several axes: <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A[I_{XY}, J] \to  A[I_Y, J]]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>

<html:p><html:em>Mechanism</html:em>: pass each shard around the <fr:link href="/UEA0/" title="Ring topology" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/UEA0/" display-uri="UEA0" type="local">ring</fr:link>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N-1]]></fr:tex> hops in one direction or about <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N/2]]></fr:tex> bidirectionally.
</html:p>

<html:p><html:em>Cost</html:em>, when bandwidth-bound: <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T = V / W_{\mathrm {ici}}]]></fr:tex> on bidirectional bandwidth, or <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V / (W_{\mathrm {ici}} \cdot  N_{\mathrm {axes}})]]></fr:tex> across several mesh axes. The takeaway is that this is <html:strong>independent of the device count <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex></html:strong> — each hop sends <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V/X]]></fr:tex> bytes but you make <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X/2]]></fr:tex> hops. Only array size and link bandwidth matter.
</html:p>

<html:p><html:em>Latency-bound regime</html:em>: about <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[1\mu \mathrm {s}]]></fr:tex> intrinsic cost per hop, so <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T = \max (T_{\min } \cdot  \sum _i |X_i| / 2,\; V / (W \cdot  N_{\mathrm {axes}}))]]></fr:tex>. On v5e (<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[4.5e10]]></fr:tex> unidirectional) anything under ~45 kB is latency-bound.
</html:p>
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree><fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>12</fr:day></fr:date><fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/JO4V/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>JO4V</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/JO4V/</fr:route><fr:title text="Gather before, or reduce after">Gather before, or reduce after</fr:title><fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="anki">1786546736036</fr:meta><fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p><fr:link href="/XGI0/" title="Sharding" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/XGI0/" display-uri="XGI0" type="local">Sharded</fr:link> matmul, case 2: one multiplicand has a sharded contracting dimension, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A[I, J_X] \cdot  B[J, K] \to  C[I, K]]]></fr:tex>. Which primitive, and what is the alternative?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  <html:strong><fr:link href="/VSDZ/" title="AllGather" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/VSDZ/" display-uri="VSDZ" type="local">AllGather</fr:link> the sharded input first, then matmul locally</html:strong>: <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {AllGather}_X(A[I, J_X]) \to  A[I, J]]]></fr:tex>, then <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A[I, J] \cdot  B[J, K] \to  C[I, K]]]></fr:tex>. You cannot just multiply the local shards, because the sum over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[J]]></fr:tex> is split across <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> and each device holds only a partial sum.

  <html:strong>The alternative</html:strong> is to treat it like <fr:link href="/KIRV/" title="Matching contracting shards give partial sums" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/KIRV/" display-uri="KIRV" type="local">case 3</fr:link>: do the local partial matmul and <fr:link href="/U351/" title="AllReduce" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/U351/" display-uri="U351" type="local">AllReduce</fr:link> or <fr:link href="/CAGW/" title="ReduceScatter" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/CAGW/" display-uri="CAGW" type="local">ReduceScatter</fr:link> the result. That shards the FLOPs but usually costs more comms. For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X[B,D] \cdot  Y[D_X,F]]]></fr:tex>, the gather moves <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2DF]]></fr:tex> bytes and the reduce moves <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2 \cdot  2BF]]></fr:tex>, so the reduce path wins roughly when <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[D > 2B]]></fr:tex>.

  Rare in practice, though — the other input is usually sharded along the same axis anyway, as under FSDP.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree><fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>12</fr:day></fr:date><fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/QBBA/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>QBBA</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/QBBA/</fr:route><fr:title text="A mesh axis may appear only once">A mesh axis may appear only once</fr:title><fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="anki">1786546736336</fr:meta><fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p><fr:link href="/XGI0/" title="Sharding" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/XGI0/" display-uri="XGI0" type="local">Sharded</fr:link> matmul, case 4: both multiplicands have a <html:em>non-contracting</html:em> dimension sharded along the same mesh axis, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A[I_X, J] \cdot  B[J, K_X]]]></fr:tex>. Why is this invalid, and how do you resolve it?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  A mesh axis may appear <html:strong>at most once</html:strong> in a sharding. Writing <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C[I_X, K_X]]]></fr:tex> would give shard <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[i]]></fr:tex> only the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(i,i)]]></fr:tex> block — the block diagonal. The off-diagonal blocks of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex> exist nowhere, so the result is unrecoverable.

  <html:strong>The fix</html:strong> is to <fr:link href="/VSDZ/" title="AllGather" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/VSDZ/" display-uri="VSDZ" type="local">AllGather</fr:link> one of the two inputs along <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> first, so that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> appears once in the output:

  <html:ul><html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {AllGather}_X(A) \to  A[I, J]]]></fr:tex>, giving <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C[I, K_X]]]></fr:tex>.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {AllGather}_X(B) \to  B[J, K]]]></fr:tex>, giving <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C[I_X, K]]]></fr:tex>.</html:li></html:ul>

  Which one you pick is decided by the sharding the downstream ops want.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree></fr:mainmatter>
