            <fr:title text="Ring topology">Ring topology</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter><html:p>A <fr:link href="/95AC/" title="Direct vs indirect networks" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/95AC/" display-uri="95AC" type="local">direct network</fr:link> where each node connects to two neighbors, forming a closed loop. Bidirectional rings allow either direction of travel; unidirectional rings allow only one. The simplest topology to implement and reason about.</html:p><html:p>In the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k]]></fr:tex>-ary <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>-cube family, a ring is a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k]]></fr:tex>-ary 1-cube.</html:p><html:ul><html:li>Degree 2 (every node).</html:li>
  <html:li>Diameter <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lfloor  k/2 \rfloor ]]></fr:tex> for a bidirectional <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k]]></fr:tex>-node ring.</html:li>
  <html:li>Bisection bandwidth 2 — cut the ring at exactly two links.</html:li>
  <html:li>Hardware overhead very low: routers have two ports.</html:li></html:ul>
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(121, 65, 66);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(121, 65, 66);">Problem.</html:span> Worst-case hop count is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[O(k)]]></fr:tex>. Doesn't scale beyond a small number of nodes.</html:div>


 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(219, 216, 215);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(109, 103, 101);">Remark.</html:span> Despite the poor scaling, rings are common in commercial CPUs (Intel's L3 ring bus on Sandy/Ivy Bridge) because they're cheap and 8–16 cores is enough.</html:div>
</fr:mainmatter>
